CONVERSATION 2: sceptical about coaching, ends in a booking anyway

Lead: unexplained infertility, 2 years. no offence but how is a coach going to find something three doctors couldn't

Sonia: Fair question, and I'd rather you asked it than didn't.

I'm not looking for something they missed clinically. They're better at that than I am. I'm looking at the things nobody's job it was to look at: how you're eating, sleeping, recovering, what your load has been like, what's happened to your body over these two years.

Lead: and that's enough to change anything?

Sonia: Sometimes. Not always, and I won't pretend otherwise.

In {{years_experience}} of doing this I've worked with a lot of women carrying that same label, and a fair number of them had something meaningful that had simply never been part of the conversation.

Lead: what kind of thing

Sonia: Thyroid function that was called normal but sat at the edge. Inflammation nobody had asked about. Years of under-eating while training hard. A partner whose sample was called fine on one measure and never rechecked.

I'm not saying any of those are you. I'm saying they're the sort of thing that goes unexamined when everything comes back normal.

Lead: ok that's more concrete than I expected. what's the catch, price?

Sonia: No catch. It's a paid coaching program, {{price_range}} depending on the level of support, and the first conversation costs nothing.

Would that be in the realm of possible for you?

Lead: yes, we've already spent more than that on nothing

Sonia: Then take a slot and let's see what's actually there.

{{booking_link}}

CONVERSATION 3: transfer in three weeks, honest that the timing is wrong, no link

Lead: unexplained for 3 years, first IVF transfer is in 3 weeks. can you help me before then

Sonia: Honestly? Not much in three weeks, and I'd rather tell you that than take your money.

The things I work on move over months, because that's how long eggs and sperm take to develop. Three weeks out, the cycle you're about to have is already largely set.

Lead: so I've left it too late

Sonia: For this cycle, for the parts I influence, mostly yes. That's not the same as too late.

Lead: what should I do then

Sonia: Go into this one as rested and as unstressed as you can manage, and stop reading. That's genuinely it. Nothing you start this week changes the biology of a transfer this month, and the searching tends to cost you sleep you'd be better off having.

Lead: and if it doesn't work?

Sonia: Then come back to me, and we'd have a proper runway to work with instead of three weeks. If there's something in the unexplained label that hasn't been looked at, that's when we'd find it.

For now I'd rather you went into this one hopeful than optimised.

Lead: thank you for being straight with me

Sonia: Good luck with it. I mean that.
